# motoquencer2 in a `linktonext` chain: `probabilitymode` is read off the chain
# MAIN only, exactly like fadermode and buttonmode. The gate-probability lane is
# one menu the whole chain shares, so a linked member's own probabilitymode is
# ignored (the rule the manual gives for fadermode on linked sequencers).
#
# The main sets probabilitymode from I3; the MEMBER hard-codes 1 (random chances).
# fadermode 12 hands the faders to the member (12 / 10 = chain index 1) and puts
# them on its own gate-probability lane (12 - 10 = 2).
#
# With the main at 2 the member's lane must have the MAIN's four trig notches:
#   0.40 -> round(0.40*3) = 1 -> 1/3 = 0.333333, stored 4 = "every odd turn"
# If the member's own `probabilitymode = 1` were honoured the five-notch grid would
# snap 0.40 to 0.5 and store 3 instead. Widening the MAIN to 0 then reads the stored
# value off the eight-notch grid as 4/7 = 0.571429 (the member's 1 would have left
# 1/7 = 0.142857 there).
experimental
tickrate 100
patch <<<
[m4]
[motoquencer2]
    numfaders       = 4
    linktonext      = 1
    fadermode       = 12
    probabilitymode = I3
    clock           = I1
    cv              = O1
[motoquencer2]
    probabilitymode = 1
    cv              = O3
>>>
@0 set I3 2
@1 move F1 0.4
@1 expect F1 0.333333 tol 1e-4
@2 expect F1 0.333333 tol 1e-4
# tick3: the MAIN widens to all eight notches — the member follows it, not its own
# input, and the value it stored is the trig table's 4.
@3 set I3 0
@3 expect F1 0.571429 tol 1e-4
@4 expect F1 0.571429 tol 1e-4
